\title[The trace of a semiprime is the least hidden symmetric invariant]
{The trace of a semiprime is the least hidden symmetric invariant\\
\normalsize Exactly one bit per prime, the Legendre symbol as the visible bit,
the low-bit law $s_1 = 1 - N_1$, and a counting barrier}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{15 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $N = pq$ be a semiprime with odd prime factors and let $s = p+q$ be its \emph{trace}. The
pair $(N,s)$ determines $\{p,q\}$ as the roots of $X^2 - sX + N$, so the trace is the minimal
factor-bearing symmetric witness of the factorisation. We describe completely how much the
public modulus constrains the residues of $s$, and contrast this with the corresponding
question for the factor $p$.

Three phenomena are established. \emph{Factor invisibility}: modulo an odd prime $\ell$ the set
of residues a factor of a nonzero $N$ may occupy is the full unit group $\FF_\ell^\times$, and the
counting form of statistical independence holds exactly, so $I(p \bmod \ell\,;\,N \bmod \ell) = 0$
identically. \emph{Trace visibility, quantified exactly}: the trace set
$S_\ell(N) = \{x+y : xy = N\}$ equals $\{s : s^2 - 4N \text{ is a square}\}$ and satisfies
$2|S_\ell(N)| = \ell + \chi_\ell(N)$ with $\chi_\ell$ the quadratic character; the trace set is
multiplicative across coprime moduli, so for squarefree odd $M$ coprime to $N$ its density is
$2^{-\omega(M)}$ up to corrections $1 \pm 1/\ell$ --- exactly one bit per prime, additively
independent. At the prime $2$ the constraint becomes an identity: $p + q + pq \equiv 3 \pmod 4$
for all odd $p,q$, that is $s_1 = 1 - N_1$; this is sharp, since no analogous law holds modulo
$8$ (bit $2$ obeys only a $3/4$ law). \emph{The visible information is public and does not
scale}: the deviation of $|S_\ell(N)|$ from $\ell/2$ is exactly the Legendre symbol
$\left(\frac{N}{\ell}\right)$, computable from $N$ in polynomial time, so the trace-set size sees
$N$ only through that public bit; and congruence data modulo $M = \prod_{\ell \in P}\ell$ leaves
at least $\big(\prod(\ell-1)\big)(B/M - 1)/2^{|P|}$ candidate traces in a window $[1,B]$, so
pinning $s$ requires $|P| \gtrsim \log_2 N$ primes and hence a modulus far exceeding $N$.
Finally the phenomenon is strictly an arity-$2$ effect: the two-factor trace set is always a
proper subset of $\FF_\ell$, whereas the three-factor sum set $\{x+y+z : xyz = N\}$ is already all
of $\FF_{11}$ for every invertible $N$, with $\ell \in \{3,5,7\}$ the only exceptions.
\end{abstract}

