\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}
The security of factoring-based cryptography is usually discussed in the language of
computational complexity. A parallel and more elementary question is rarely asked in a sharp
form: \emph{how much information about a factorisation is present in the public modulus at all?}

Fix a semiprime $N = pq$ with $p,q$ odd primes. There are two natural hidden quantities: the
\emph{factor} $p$, which is asymmetric, and the \emph{trace} $s = p+q$, which is symmetric.

The trace deserves attention because it is exactly as good as the factorisation. Given $(N,s)$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:quad}
X^2 - sX + N = 0, \qquad p,q = \frac{s \pm \sqrt{s^2-4N}}{2},
\end{equation}
so $s$ is a \emph{minimal factor-bearing symmetric witness}: one number, symmetric in the
factors, from which the factorisation follows by a single square root. Any residue-based attack
that hopes to avoid asymmetry must target $s$ or something like it. The natural probes are
congruences: what does $N \bmod m$ say about $s \bmod m$, and about $p \bmod m$?

\subsection{The empirical picture}
A large computational study over semiprime pairs with $k$-bit factors ($k = 12,14$; between
$32\,640$ and $380\,628$ pairs; mutual information estimated modulo $m$; Chinese-Remainder
trace-set sizes; full pairwise bit matrices) produced the following readings.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $I(p \bmod m\,;\,N \bmod m) \approx 0$ for every modulus tested --- a ``zero block''.
\item $I(s \bmod m\,;\,N \bmod m) = 1.0000$ bit at $m = 3$ and $1.03$--$1.06$ bits at larger odd
primes, tracking the trace-set size $\approx (m+1)/2$.
\item Jointly, $|S_M(N)|/M = 2^{-\omega(M)}$ for primorial $M$: measured $0.5011$, $0.2509$,
$0.1260$.
\item An exact low-bit relation $s_1 = 1 - N_1$ on $300\,000$ of $300\,000$ sampled pairs, and a
partial relation at bit $2$ with probability $0.754$.
\item In the pairwise bit matrix, $2.32$ of the trace's $H(s) = 12.6$ bits are visible
($18.5$--$21.9\%$), versus about $5\%$ for the factor.
\end{enumerate}
This paper proves the exact theorems behind items 1--4, identifies precisely \emph{which} bit is
visible in item 2, explains item 5 structurally, and proves the negative result that makes the
profile harmless.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout $R$ is a finite commutative ring with identity and $\ell$ an odd prime. We write
$\FF_\ell = \ZZ/\ell\ZZ$ and $\chi_\ell$ for the quadratic character: $\chi_\ell(N) = +1$ if $N$ is a
nonzero square mod $\ell$, $-1$ if a non-square, $0$ if $\ell \mid N$.

\begin{definition}[Factor pairs]
For $N \in R$, $\FP_R(N) = \{(x,y) \in R \times R : xy = N\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Trace set]
The \emph{trace set} of $N \in R$ is $S_R(N) = \{x+y : xy = N\} \subseteq R$. For
$R = \ZZ/m\ZZ$ we write $S_m(N)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Factor set]
The \emph{factor set} of $N \in R$ is $F_R(N) = \{x : \exists y,\ xy = N\}$.
\end{definition}

The trace set is the complete list of residues the true trace of a factorisation of $N$ can
occupy; likewise $F_m(N)$ for the factor. Constraint on the hidden quantity is exactly the
statement that these sets are small.

\begin{definition}[Triple sum set]
$T_R(N) = \{x+y+z : xyz = N\} \subseteq R$, the arity-$3$ analogue of the trace set.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Unit-pair model]
For a prime $\ell$ let $U_\ell = \FF_\ell^\times \times \FF_\ell^\times$, of cardinality
$(\ell-1)^2$, model the pair of factor residues of a random semiprime coprime to $\ell$. Two
events \emph{satisfy the product rule} when
$\#\{\text{joint}\}\cdot|U_\ell| = \#\{\text{first}\}\cdot\#\{\text{second}\}$, which is exactly
the vanishing of the corresponding contribution to mutual information.
\end{definition}

\section{Factor invisibility: an exact zero}

\begin{theorem}[Factor invisibility]\label{thm:factorinv}
Let $\ell$ be a prime and $N \in \FF_\ell$, $N \neq 0$. Then $F_{\FF_\ell}(N) = \FF_\ell^\times$,
of cardinality $\ell - 1$; moreover for each $a \in \FF_\ell^\times$ and $b \in \FF_\ell$ there is
exactly one $y$ with $ay = b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $xy = N \neq 0$ then $x \neq 0$; conversely for $x \neq 0$ the element $y = Nx^{-1}$ satisfies
$xy = N$. Uniqueness of the cofactor is invertibility of $a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact independence]\label{thm:indep}
In the unit-pair model, for all $a,b \in \FF_\ell^\times$,
\[
\#\{(x,y) \in U_\ell : x = a,\ xy = b\}\cdot|U_\ell|
= \#\{(x,y) \in U_\ell : x = a\}\cdot\#\{(x,y) \in U_\ell : xy = b\}.
\]
Consequently $I(p \bmod \ell\,;\,N \bmod \ell) = 0$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The joint event is the single point $(a, a^{-1}b)$, of count $1$. The first marginal is
$\{a\}\times\FF_\ell^\times$, of size $\ell - 1$. The second marginal is the image of the
injective map $x \mapsto (x, x^{-1}b)$ on $\FF_\ell^\times$, also of size $\ell - 1$. Since
$|U_\ell| = (\ell-1)^2$, both sides equal $(\ell-1)^2$.
\end{proof}

Every term of the mutual-information sum vanishes, so the zero is exact, not a small-sample
artefact: the public residue of a semiprime is a perfect one-time pad for the residues of its
factors, at every modulus and every scale.

\section{The trace set: structure, exact size, joint law}

\subsection{The discriminant description}

\begin{theorem}[Discriminant description]\label{thm:discrim}
Let $\ell$ be an odd prime and $N,s \in \FF_\ell$. Then $s \in S_\ell(N)$ if and only if
$s^2 - 4N$ is a square in $\FF_\ell$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $s = x+y$ with $xy = N$ then $(x-y)^2 = s^2 - 4N$. Conversely if $s^2 - 4N = t^2$, put
$x = (s+t)/2$, $y = (s-t)/2$ (legitimate as $2$ is invertible); then $x+y = s$ and
$xy = (s^2-t^2)/4 = N$.
\end{proof}

The trace obeys exactly one quadratic condition, hence can be constrained by exactly one bit.

\subsection{The exact size over a prime field}

\begin{theorem}[Exact trace-set size]\label{thm:size}
Let $\ell$ be an odd prime and $N \in \FF_\ell^\times$. Then
\[
2\,|S_\ell(N)| = \ell + \chi_\ell(N) =
\begin{cases} \ell+1, & N \text{ a nonzero square},\\ \ell-1, & N \text{ a non-square}.\end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $N \neq 0$, every factorisation has $x \neq 0$ and $y = N/x$, so
$S_\ell(N) = \{x + Nx^{-1} : x \in \FF_\ell^\times\}$ is the image of a map $\varphi$ on a set of
size $\ell-1$. For $s \in S_\ell(N)$ realised as $x_0+y_0$ with $x_0y_0 = N$, the equation
$x + Nx^{-1} = s$ is equivalent to $x^2 - sx + N = 0$, whose root set is $\{x_0,y_0\}$; the fibre
therefore has two elements unless $x_0 = y_0$, i.e.\ unless $s^2 = 4N$, when it has one. Hence
\[
\ell - 1 = \#\{s \in S_\ell(N) : s^2 = 4N\} + 2\,\#\{s \in S_\ell(N) : s^2 \neq 4N\}.
\]
If $N = r^2$ with $r \neq 0$, then $s^2 = 4N$ factors as $(s-2r)(s+2r) = 0$ with $2r \neq -2r$,
giving exactly two degenerate traces, both realised (by $r\cdot r$ and $(-r)(-r)$); if $N$ is a
non-square there are none, since $s^2 = 4N$ would exhibit $N = (s/2)^2$. Writing $d \in \{0,2\}$
for that count and $c = |S_\ell(N)|$ we get $\ell - 1 = 2c - d$.
\end{proof}

In particular $|S_\ell(N)| < \ell$: the trace set is always a proper subset, and
$\log_2\big(\ell/|S_\ell(N)|\big) = 1 + O(1/\ell)$. This refines the experimental reading
$(m+1)/2$: the true value is $(m + \chi_m(N))/2$.

\subsection{Multiplicativity}

\begin{theorem}[Product rings]\label{thm:prod}
For finite commutative rings $R,S$ and $N \in R$, $M \in S$,
\[
S_{R\times S}\big((N,M)\big) = S_R(N)\times S_S(M),\qquad
|S_{R\times S}((N,M))| = |S_R(N)|\cdot|S_S(M)|.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A factorisation $(x_1,x_2)(y_1,y_2) = (N,M)$ is precisely a pair of factorisations
$x_1y_1 = N$, $x_2y_2 = M$, and sums are computed componentwise.
\end{proof}

The trace set is also transported by any ring isomorphism $e : R \to S$, in the form
$S_S(e(N)) = e\big(S_R(N)\big)$. With the Chinese Remainder isomorphism:

\begin{corollary}[CRT multiplicativity]\label{cor:crt}
For coprime $m,n \ge 1$ and $N \in \ZZ$, $|S_{mn}(N)| = |S_m(N)|\cdot|S_n(N)|$; by induction,
$|S_M(N)| = \prod_{\ell \in P}|S_\ell(N)|$ for squarefree $M = \prod_{\ell \in P}\ell$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The joint law: one bit per prime}

\begin{theorem}[One bit per prime, additively independent]\label{thm:onebit}
Let $P$ be a finite set of odd primes, $M = \prod_{\ell \in P}\ell$, and $N$ an integer divisible
by no $\ell \in P$. Then
\[
\prod_{\ell \in P}(\ell-1) \;\le\; 2^{|P|}\,|S_M(N)| \;\le\; \prod_{\ell \in P}(\ell+1).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:crt} and Theorem~\ref{thm:size},
$2^{|P|}|S_M(N)| = \prod_{\ell\in P}\big(\ell + \chi_\ell(N)\big)$, and each factor lies in
$\{\ell-1,\ell+1\}$.
\end{proof}

Dividing by $M$,
\[
\frac{|S_M(N)|}{M} = 2^{-|P|}\prod_{\ell \in P}\Big(1 + \frac{\chi_\ell(N)}{\ell}\Big)
= 2^{-\omega(M)}\big(1 + O(\textstyle\sum_\ell 1/\ell)\big),
\]
the measured law $0.5011$, $0.2509$, $0.1260,\dots$ The information content is
$\log_2\big(M/|S_M(N)|\big) = \omega(M) + O(1)$: the bits are additive across primes with no
interaction.

\subsection{The size contrast}

\begin{theorem}[Trace bit versus factor bits]\label{thm:contrast}
For an odd prime $\ell$ and $N \in \FF_\ell^\times$, $2|S_\ell(N)| \le |F_{\FF_\ell}(N)| + 2 = \ell+1$;
and for $\ell \ge 5$ strictly $|S_\ell(N)| < |F_{\FF_\ell}(N)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[The trace is not information-free]\label{prop:notindep}
In the unit-pair model at $\ell = 5$, with trace value $s = 2$ and product value $b = 1$, the
product rule fails: $1\cdot 16 \neq 3\cdot 4$. Hence $I(s \bmod 5\,;\,N \bmod 5) > 0$.
\end{proposition}

\section{Which bit is visible? The Legendre symbol}

\begin{theorem}[The visible bit is the quadratic character]\label{thm:leg}
For an odd prime $\ell$ and an integer $a$ with $\ell \nmid a$,
\[
2\,|S_\ell(a)| = \ell + \left(\frac{a}{\ell}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Character-indistinguishability]\label{cor:indist}
If $\ell \nmid ab$ and $\left(\frac{a}{\ell}\right) = \left(\frac{b}{\ell}\right)$, then
$|S_\ell(a)| = |S_\ell(b)|$.
\end{corollary}

The significance is cryptographic. The Legendre symbol $\left(\frac{N}{\ell}\right)$ is computable
from $N$ alone in polynomial time by quadratic reciprocity, with no knowledge of $p$ or $q$.
Hence the single bit the trace constraint exposes at each prime is \emph{public data}, and by
Corollary~\ref{cor:indist} the size of the constraint distinguishes two moduli only when their
characters differ. The trace is the least hidden symmetric invariant, but what is visible about
it is the shadow of a quantity that was never hidden.

This also explains item 5 of the empirical picture: the visible $2.32$ bits decompose into the
exact low-bit relations of the next section, which are functions of $N \bmod 4$, and a thin
sliver at the top of the expansion from the carry-out of $p+q$ --- both symmetric, both
public-derivable, neither isolating $p$ or $q$.

\section{The exact low-bit law}

\begin{theorem}[Exact low-bit theorem, sharp form]\label{thm:lowbit}
For all odd integers $p,q$: $\;p + q + pq \equiv 3 \pmod 4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
With $p = 2a+1$, $q = 2b+1$ one has $p+q+pq = 4(a+b+ab)+3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Bit form]\label{cor:bitform}
For odd $p,q$, with $N = pq$ and $s = p+q$,
\[
s \bmod 4 = \begin{cases} 2, & N \equiv 1 \pmod 4,\\ 0, & N \equiv 3 \pmod 4,\end{cases}
\qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad s_1 = 1 - N_1,
\]
where $x_1 = \lfloor x/2\rfloor \bmod 2$ denotes bit $1$: bit $1$ of the trace is the complement
of bit $1$ of the modulus.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:lowbit} gives $s \equiv 3 - N \pmod 4$; odd $N$ has $N \bmod 4 \in \{1,3\}$, and
$s$ is even, which pins $s \bmod 4$ to $2$ or $0$. Bit $1$ of an even $s$ is $(s \bmod 4)/2$ and
bit $1$ of an odd $N$ is $(N \bmod 4 - 1)/2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Zero marginal information]\label{cor:zeroinfo}
If $p,q,p',q'$ are odd with $pq \equiv p'q' \pmod 4$, then $p+q \equiv p'+q' \pmod 4$.
\end{corollary}

The exactly-visible bit of the trace is a deterministic function of the public modulus; it
carries no information about the factorisation.

\subsection{Sharpness}

\begin{theorem}[No law at bit 2]\label{thm:sharp}
There exist odd pairs with the same $N \bmod 8$ but different $s \bmod 8$: $(3,3)$ gives
$N = 9 \equiv 1$, $s = 6$, while $(5,13)$ gives $N = 65 \equiv 1 \pmod 8$, $s = 18 \equiv 2
\pmod 8$. Hence no relation $s \bmod 8 = f(N \bmod 8)$ holds.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The $3/4$ law]\label{thm:threequarters}
In the uniform model over pairs of odd residues modulo $8$ --- all that $s \bmod 8$ and
$N \bmod 8$ depend on --- bit $2$ of the trace differs from bit $2$ of the modulus for exactly
$12$ of the $16$ pairs, i.e.\ with probability $3/4$.
\end{theorem}

The experiment measured $0.754$. Bit $0$ of $s$ is forced ($s$ is even), bit $1$ is forced by
$N$, and from bit $2$ onwards only probabilities remain.

\subsection{The \texorpdfstring{$k$}{k}-factor generalisation}

\begin{theorem}[$k$-factor low-bit law]\label{thm:kfactor}
Let $a_1,\dots,a_k$ be odd integers, $N = \prod_i a_i$ and $e_1 = \sum_i a_i$. Then
\[
e_1 + 1 \equiv N + k \pmod 4 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on the length of the list of factors. For the empty list $e_1 = 0$, $N = 1$, $k = 0$
and $0+1 \equiv 1+0$. For the step, write the head as $a = 2x+1$ and the (odd) product of the
tail as $\Pi = 2y+1$; then $a\Pi = 4xy + 2x + 2y + 1$. Combining this with the inductive
hypothesis for the tail and $e_1^{\mathrm{new}} = a + e_1^{\mathrm{tail}}$, the claim follows by
linear arithmetic modulo $4$.
\end{proof}

For $k = 2$ this is Theorem~\ref{thm:lowbit} rearranged. The exactly-visible low bit of the first
symmetric function is a function of the product and the \emph{number} of factors --- never of
the factors themselves.

\section{Arity: the trace constraint is a two-factor phenomenon}

\begin{proposition}[Arity monotonicity]\label{prop:mono}
For any finite commutative ring $R$ and $N \in R$, $|S_R(N)| \le |T_R(N)|$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
If $xy = N$ then $xy\cdot 1 = N$, so the injective translate $s \mapsto s+1$ maps $S_R(N)$ into
$T_R(N)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Properness at arity 2]\label{thm:proper}
For every odd prime $\ell$ and $N \in \FF_\ell^\times$, $S_\ell(N) \neq \FF_\ell$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$|S_\ell(N)| \le (\ell+1)/2 < \ell$ for $\ell \ge 3$, by Theorem~\ref{thm:size}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Arity-3 collapse at $\ell = 11$]\label{thm:collapse}
For every $N \in \FF_{11}^\times$, $T_{11}(N) = \FF_{11}$: the three-factor sum carries zero bits
at $\ell = 11$, in contrast to the one bit of the two-factor trace.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Arity dichotomy]\label{cor:dichotomy}
For every $N \in \FF_{11}^\times$: $S_{11}(N) \subsetneq \FF_{11}$ while $T_{11}(N) = \FF_{11}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Exception at $\ell = 5$]\label{thm:five}
$T_5(1) \neq \FF_5$: over $\FF_5$, no triple with product $1$ sums to $2$.
\end{theorem}

Computationally, $\ell = 3,5,7$ are the only primes at which the arity-$3$ sum set fails to be
everything; from $11$ upwards the collapse is total (verified to $\ell \le 19$).

\begin{remark}[Why $11$?]
Asking $s \in T_\ell(N)$ means solving $xyz = N$, $x+y+z = s$. Eliminating, one seeks $z \neq 0$
with $x+y = s-z$, $xy = N/z$, i.e.\ with $(s-z)^2 - 4N/z$ a square; clearing denominators,
\[
w^2 = z^2(s-z)^2 - 4Nz,
\]
a quartic model of a genus-$1$ curve. The Hasse--Weil bound gives $\ell + O(\sqrt{\ell})$ points,
so a point with $z \neq 0$ exists once $\ell$ is large enough, and the numerics say ``large
enough'' means $\ell \ge 11$. The contrast is that between a \emph{conic} (arity $2$: one
quadratic condition, half the residues, one bit) and an \emph{elliptic curve} (arity $3$: enough
points to hit every $s$, zero bits).
\end{remark}

\section{The pinning barrier}

\begin{lemma}[Search window]\label{lem:window}
If $p,q \ge 2$ then $p+q \le pq = N$; so the trace lies in $[1,N]$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$(p-1)(q-1) \ge 1$ rearranges to $pq \ge p+q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Residue classes meet the window]\label{lem:class}
For $M \ge 1$, $B \ge 0$ and any $s \in \ZZ/M\ZZ$,
$\#\{t \in [1,B] : t \equiv s \ (\mathrm{mod}\ M)\} \ge \lfloor B/M\rfloor - 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $K = \lfloor B/M\rfloor$; if $K < 2$ the bound is vacuous. Otherwise the $K-1$ integers
$s^{*} + (k+1)M$ for $0 \le k < K-1$, with $s^{*} \in [0,M)$ the least representative, are
distinct and lie in $[1,B]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Residue sets]\label{lem:sets}
For any $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \ZZ/M\ZZ$,
$\#\{t \in [1,B] : t \bmod M \in \mathcal{S}\} \ge |\mathcal{S}|\big(\lfloor B/M\rfloor - 1\big)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Pinning barrier]\label{thm:barrier}
Let $P$ be a finite set of odd primes, $M = \prod_{\ell \in P}\ell$, and $N$ coprime to $M$. Then
\[
\#\{t \in [1,B] : t \bmod M \in S_M(N)\}
\;\ge\; \frac{\big(\prod_{\ell \in P}(\ell-1)\big)\big(\lfloor B/M\rfloor - 1\big)}{2^{|P|}} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:sets} with $\mathcal{S} = S_M(N)$ and bound $|S_M(N)|$ from below by
Theorem~\ref{thm:onebit}.
\end{proof}

Since $\prod_{\ell\in P}(\ell-1) = M\prod(1-1/\ell)$, the right side is $\asymp B\,2^{-|P|}$ for
$B \gg M$: after applying all congruence information modulo $M$, the surviving candidate set
still has density about $2^{-\omega(M)}$. To reduce $[1,N]$ to a single candidate one needs
$2^{|P|} \gtrsim N$, i.e.\ $|P| \gtrsim \log_2 N$ primes, hence a modulus enormously larger than
$N$ --- and once $M > N$ the residue of $s$ modulo $M$ \emph{is} $s$, so the ``attack''
presupposes its own output.

\begin{proposition}[Two candidates always survive]\label{prop:two}
If $3M \le N$ then every residue class modulo $M$ contains at least two integers of $[1,N]$; in
particular no modulus $M \le N/3$ can determine the trace.
\end{proposition}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Summary of the profile}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}
\toprule
Quantity & Residues available mod $\ell$ & Bits revealed & Nature of visible information\\
\midrule
Factor $p$ & all $\ell-1$ units & $0$ exactly & none\\
Trace $s = p+q$ & $(\ell+\chi_\ell(N))/2$ & $1$ per prime, additive & Legendre symbol --- public\\
Trace, low bits & $s \equiv 3-N \pmod 4$ & exact identity & function of $N \bmod 4$ --- public\\
Triple sum & all $\ell$, for $\ell \ge 11$ & $0$ & none\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The trace is the least hidden symmetric invariant: the unique row where the public modulus
constrains a factor-bearing quantity at all. Yet wherever information appears, it is information
a reader of $N$ already possessed.

\subsection{Size versus set}

Theorem~\ref{thm:leg} says the \emph{size} of $S_\ell(N)$ sees $N$ only through $\chi_\ell(N)$.
The \emph{set} appears to see everything.

\begin{conjecture}[Trace set is a complete invariant]\label{conj:C1}
For every odd prime $\ell$ and all $N,N' \in \FF_\ell^\times$, $S_\ell(N) = S_\ell(N')$ implies
$N = N'$.
\end{conjecture}

This is verified exhaustively at $\ell = 13$ and computationally for $\ell \le 41$. The heuristic
uses Theorem~\ref{thm:discrim}: $S_\ell(N) = \{s : \chi_\ell(s^2-4N) \neq -1\}$ is the
non-residue-avoiding locus of a quadratic pencil, and
\[
|S_\ell(N)\cap S_\ell(N')| = \tfrac{1}{4}\sum_s\big(1+\chi(s^2-4N)\big)\big(1+\chi(s^2-4N')\big) + O(1)
\]
is $\ell/4 + O(\sqrt{\ell})$ by Weil's bound when $N \neq N'$, whereas equality of the sets would
force $\ell/2 + O(1)$. Knowing $|S_\ell(N)|$ is worth one public bit; knowing $S_\ell(N)$ is worth
all of $N$ --- which was public. The chain never touches $p$.

\begin{conjecture}[Arity-3 threshold]\label{conj:C2}
For every prime $\ell \ge 11$ and every $N \in \FF_\ell^\times$, $\{x+y+z : xyz = N\} = \FF_\ell$,
and $\ell = 3,5,7$ are the only exceptions.
\end{conjecture}

\subsection{What this is not}

None of these results is progress toward factoring, and Theorem~\ref{thm:barrier} is the proof
that they cannot be. Their value is the opposite: a negative result made quantitative. Where
security arguments usually say ``we know of no efficient algorithm'', here one can say, of a
specific and maximally favourable target, exactly how much information exists ($\omega(M)$ bits),
exactly what it consists of (Legendre symbols and $N \bmod 4$), and exactly why it does not
accumulate fast enough (density $2^{-\omega}$ against a window of size $N$).

\subsection{Further directions}

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Higher symmetric functions.} For $k$ factors the functions $e_1,\dots,e_{k-1}$
interpolate between the trace and the full factorisation. Which are congruence-constrained, and
by how much? Theorem~\ref{thm:kfactor} handles $e_1$ modulo $4$ for all $k$; the arity dichotomy
handles $e_1$ modulo $\ell$ for $k \le 3$.
\item \emph{Prime-power moduli.} Modulo $\ell^2$ the trace set should have size
$\ell(\ell+\chi)/2$ by Hensel lifting away from the ramified locus, with corrections at the
degenerate traces $s^2 = 4N$.
\item \emph{The carry sliver.} Quantify how many high bits of $s$ are determined by $N$ when
$p,q$ lie in a known range; this is a question about the distribution of $\sqrt{N}$-sized
quantities.
\item \emph{Other symmetric witnesses.} Does $p^2+q^2 = s^2 - 2N$ or $|p-q| = \sqrt{s^2-4N}$ carry
a different profile? The first is a function of $s$ and $N$, hence carries the same bits; the
second is the discriminant itself.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

For a semiprime $N = pq$, the factor $p$ is congruence-invisible: modulo every prime, the public
residue of $N$ excludes no candidate, and the independence is exact. The trace $s = p+q$ is
congruence-visible: it is confined modulo each odd prime $\ell$ to a set of size exactly
$(\ell + \chi_\ell(N))/2$, the halvings are perfectly multiplicative across primes, and at the
prime $2$ the constraint sharpens into the identity $s_1 = 1 - N_1$, sharp because bit $2$ obeys
only a $3/4$ law. The visible bit at each odd prime is precisely the Legendre symbol of $N$ ---
public data. The whole effect is an arity-$2$ phenomenon: the three-factor sum set is all of
$\FF_\ell$ from $\ell = 11$ onwards. And congruence data modulo $M$ leaves $\asymp N2^{-\omega(M)}$
candidate traces in $[1,N]$, so pinning the trace demands more primes than the modulus has bits.

The trace is the least-hidden symmetric invariant of a semiprime, the most accessible residue
target that exists --- and it is not a factoring tool.

